syntax:
Using directive:

using namespace std;

Using declaration:

using std::memberName;

Access a member directly using the scope resolution operator:

std::memberName

examples:
Use a using directive so std:: is not required:

using namespace std;

string name;
cout << "Enter your name: ";
cin >> name;

Without using namespace std, prefix names from the std namespace with std:::

std::string name;
std::cout << "Enter your name: ";
std::cin >> name;

Use using declarations to make specific names available:

using std::cout;
using std::endl;

cout << "Hello" << endl;

Only the selected names are made available without std::. Other names from the std namespace still require std:: or their own using declaration.

syntax:
if(condition)
{
    statement;
}

examples:
Indent statements inside an if statement.
if(grade >= 70)
{
    cout << "Passing" << endl;
}

Each nested block is indented an additional level.
for(int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
{
    if(i % 2 == 0)
    {
        cout << i << endl;
    }
}

Poor indentation makes the structure of the program difficult to see.
if(grade >= 70)
{
cout << "Passing" << endl;
}

Better:
if(grade >= 70)
{
    cout << "Passing" << endl;
}

syntax:
{
    statements;
}

examples:
An if statement containing a code block.
if(age >= 18)
{
    cout << "Adult" << endl;
    status = true;
}

A variable declared inside a code block has block scope.
{
    int number = 10;
    cout << number << endl;
}
// number cannot be accessed here

Code blocks can be nested.
if(score >= 70)
{
    cout << "Passing" << endl;

    if(score >= 90)
    {
        cout << "Excellent" << endl;
    }
}

syntax:
{
    statements;
}

examples:
Curly braces define the code block of an if statement.
if(temperature > 100)
{
    cout << "Too hot" << endl;
}

Curly braces define the body of a function.
void showMessage()
{
    cout << "Hello" << endl;
}

Each opening curly brace must have a matching closing curly brace.
for(int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
{
    if(i % 2 == 0)
    {
        cout << i << endl;
    }
}

