examples:
Declare an empty vector:

vector<int> scores;

The vector initially contains no elements.

Declare a vector with five elements:

vector<int> scores(5);

The vector contains five int elements, each initialized to 0.

Declare and initialize a vector:

vector<int> scores = {85, 92, 78, 96, 88};

The vector contains five elements.

Access elements using the subscript operator:

vector<int> scores = {85, 92, 78, 96, 88};

cout << scores[0] << endl;
cout << scores[2] << endl;

scores[0] contains 85.
scores[2] contains 78.

Access elements using at():

vector<int> scores = {85, 92, 78, 96, 88};

cout << scores.at(0) << endl;
cout << scores.at(2) << endl;

Modify an element:

scores[2] = 80;

The value at index 2 is changed from 78 to 80.

Use a variable as the index:

int index = 3;
cout << scores[index] << endl;

Correct array traversal:

for(int index = 0; index < 5; index++)
{
    cout << scores[index] << endl;
}

Incorrect traversal condition:

for(int index = 0; index <= 5; index++)
{
    cout << scores[index] << endl;
}

When index becomes 5, the program attempts to access an element outside the array.

Vector access using at():

vector<int> scores = {85, 92, 78, 96, 88};

cout << scores.at(2) << endl;

If an invalid index is passed to at(), an exception is thrown.

syntax:
Get the current size:

vectorName.size();

Change the size:

vectorName.resize(newSize);

Increase the size and initialize new elements:

vectorName.resize(newSize, initialValue);

Remove all elements:

vectorName.clear();

examples:
Get the number of elements:

vector<int> numbers = {10, 20, 30};

cout << numbers.size() << endl;

The output is 3 because the vector contains three elements.

Increase the size of a vector:

vector<int> numbers = {10, 20, 30};

numbers.resize(5);

The vector now contains:
10, 20, 30, 0, 0

The size is now 5.

Increase the size and provide an initial value:

vector<int> numbers = {10, 20, 30};

numbers.resize(5, 100);

The vector now contains:
10, 20, 30, 100, 100

Decrease the size of a vector:

vector<int> numbers = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

numbers.resize(3);

The vector now contains:
10, 20, 30

The elements 40 and 50 are removed.Remove all elements from a vector:

vector<int> numbers = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

numbers.clear();

cout << numbers.size() << endl;

The output is 0 because clear() removes all elements from the vector.

Clearing a vector does not normally reduce its capacity:

vector<int> numbers = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

numbers.clear();

cout << "Size: " << numbers.size() << endl;
cout << "Capacity: " << numbers.capacity() << endl;

The size is 0, but the vector may still have allocated storage.

syntax:
Get the current capacity:

vectorName.capacity();

Reserve storage:

vectorName.reserve(numberOfElements);

Request that unused storage be reduced:

vectorName.shrink_to_fit();

examples:
Display a vector's capacity:

vector<int> numbers;

cout << numbers.capacity() << endl;

The exact capacity is determined by the C++ implementation.

Reserve storage for 100 elements:

vector<int> numbers;

numbers.reserve(100);

cout << "Size: " << numbers.size() << endl;
cout << "Capacity: " << numbers.capacity() << endl;

The size is still 0 because reserve() does not create elements.
The capacity is at least 100.

Reserve storage before adding elements:

vector<int> numbers;

numbers.reserve(100);

for(int value = 1; value <= 100; value++)
{
    numbers.push_back(value);
}

Reserving storage in advance can reduce the number of reallocations required while the elements are added.Request that unused vector storage be reduced:

vector<int> numbers;

numbers.reserve(100);
numbers.push_back(10);
numbers.push_back(20);
numbers.push_back(30);

numbers.shrink_to_fit();

cout << "Size: " << numbers.size() << endl;
cout << "Capacity: " << numbers.capacity() << endl;

The size remains 3. shrink_to_fit() requests that unused capacity be reduced.

Clear a vector and then request that unused storage be reduced:

vector<int> numbers = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

numbers.clear();
numbers.shrink_to_fit();

cout << "Size: " << numbers.size() << endl;
cout << "Capacity: " << numbers.capacity() << endl;

clear() removes the elements, while shrink_to_fit() requests that unused storage be released.

syntax:
Add an element to the end:

vectorName.push_back(value);

Remove the last element:

vectorName.pop_back();

Insert an element at a specified position:

vectorName.insert(vectorName.begin() + index, value);

Remove an element at a specified position:

vectorName.erase(vectorName.begin() + index);

examples:
Add an element to the end of a vector:

vector<int> numbers = {10, 20, 30};

numbers.push_back(40);

The vector now contains:
10, 20, 30, 40

Remove the last element of a vector:

vector<int> numbers = {10, 20, 30, 40};

numbers.pop_back();

The vector now contains:
10, 20, 30

Insert an element at index 2:

vector<int> numbers = {10, 20, 30, 40};

numbers.insert(numbers.begin() + 2, 25);

The vector now contains:
10, 20, 25, 30, 40

The elements originally at index 2 and after are shifted toward the end.

Remove the element at index 2:

vector<int> numbers = {10, 20, 30, 40};

numbers.erase(numbers.begin() + 2);

The vector now contains:
10, 20, 40

The element 30 is removed and the elements after it shift toward the beginning.

