syntax:
Define a namespace with function prototypes and function definitions inside the namespace:

namespace NamespaceName
{
    returnType functionName(dataType parameter)
    {
        // function statements
    }

    returnType functionName(dataType parameter1, dataType parameter2)
    {
        // function statements
    }
}

Define the function prototypes inside the namespace and the function definitions outside the namespace:

namespace NamespaceName
{
    returnType functionName(dataType);
    returnType functionName(dataType, dataType);
}

returnType NamespaceName::functionName(dataType parameter)
{
    // function statements
}

returnType NamespaceName::functionName(dataType parameter1, dataType parameter2)
{
    // function statements
}

examples:
Define function prototypes and function definitions inside a namespace:

namespace Geometry
{
    double circleArea(double);
    double rectangleArea(double, double);

    double circleArea(double radius)
    {
        return 3.14159 * radius * radius;
    }

    double rectangleArea(double length, double width)
    {
        return length * width;
    }
}

The function prototypes and definitions are both inside the Geometry namespace, so Geometry:: is not needed on the function definitions.

Declare function prototypes inside a namespace and define the functions outside the namespace:

namespace Geometry
{
    double circleArea(double);
    double rectangleArea(double, double);
}

double Geometry::circleArea(double radius)
{
    return 3.14159 * radius * radius;
}

double Geometry::rectangleArea(double length, double width)
{
    return length * width;
}

Because the function definitions are outside the namespace block, Geometry:: is used with each function name to identify the namespace to which the function belongs.

Two namespaces can contain functions with the same name:

namespace Geometry
{
    double calculate(double);
}

namespace Statistics
{
    double calculate(double);
}

double Geometry::calculate(double value)
{
    return value * value;
}

double Statistics::calculate(double value)
{
    return value / 2.0;
}

Both functions can be named calculate() because each belongs to a different namespace.

syntax:
