        // stop the recursion
    }
    else
    {
        // perform part of the task
        functionName(modifiedArguments);
    }
}

examples:
A recursive countdown function:

void countDown(int number)
{
    if(number == 0)
    {
        cout << "Blastoff!" << endl;
    }
    else
    {
        cout << number << endl;
        countDown(number - 1);
    }
}

The condition number == 0 is the base case. When it is false, the function displays the current number and calls itself with number - 1, moving the function closer to the base case.

syntax:
returnType functionName(parameters)
{
    if(baseCase)
    {
        // stop the recursion
    }
    else
    {
        // recursive case
        functionName(modifiedArguments);
    }
}

examples:
Identify the base case and recursive case in a countdown function:

void countDown(int number)
{
    if(number == 0)
    {
        cout << "Blastoff!" << endl;    // base case
    }
    else
    {
        cout << number << endl;
        countDown(number - 1);           // recursive case
    }
}

The base case occurs when number equals 0. At that point, the function does not call itself again. The recursive case calls countDown() with number - 1, causing each call to move closer to the base case.

A recursive call that does not move toward the base case can cause infinite recursion:

void countDown(int number)
{
    if(number == 0)
    {
        cout << "Blastoff!" << endl;
    }
    else
    {
        countDown(number + 1);   // moves away from the base case
    }
}

If countDown() begins with a positive number, adding 1 moves the argument away from the base case instead of toward it. The recursive calls continue and can eventually cause a stack overflow.

Trace a recursive countdown:

void countDown(int number)
{
    if(number == 0)
    {
        cout << "Blastoff!" << endl;
    }
    else
    {
        cout << number << endl;
        countDown(number - 1);
    }
}

Calling countDown(3) creates the following recursive calls:

countDown(3)
    ↓
countDown(2)
    ↓
countDown(1)
    ↓
countDown(0)  ← base case

After countDown(0) finishes, control returns to countDown(1), then countDown(2), and finally countDown(3).

A value-returning recursive function shows how results are produced as the calls return:

int factorial(int number)
{
    if(number <= 1)
    {
        return 1;
    }
    else
    {
        return number * factorial(number - 1);
    }
}

factorial(4)
    ↓
4 * factorial(3)
        ↓
    3 * factorial(2)
            ↓
        2 * factorial(1)
                ↓
                1  ← base case

The results then return in reverse order:

factorial(1) = 1
       ↓
factorial(2) = 2 * 1 = 2
       ↓
factorial(3) = 3 * 2 = 6
       ↓
factorial(4) = 4 * 6 = 24

syntax:
Define an enumeration:

enum EnumerationName
{
    ENUMERATOR1,
    ENUMERATOR2,
