};

examples:
Assign specific values to enumerators:

enum Status
{
    INACTIVE = 0,
    ACTIVE = 1,
    PENDING = 5
};

Values continue increasing after an explicitly assigned value:

enum Level
{
    LOW = 1,
    MEDIUM,
    HIGH
};

LOW has the value 1, MEDIUM has the value 2, and HIGH has the value 3.

syntax:
Declare an enumeration variable:

EnumerationName variableName;

Declare and initialize an enumeration variable:

EnumerationName variableName = ENUMERATOR;

Assign an enumerator later:

variableName = ENUMERATOR;

examples:
Given the following enumeration:

enum Day
{
    MONDAY,
    TUESDAY,
    WEDNESDAY,
    THURSDAY,
    FRIDAY
};

Declare a variable of type Day:

Day today;

Use an enumeration variable in a selection structure:

if(today == FRIDAY)
{
    cout << "The weekend is almost here." << endl;
}

Use an enumeration with a switch statement:

switch(today)
{
    case MONDAY:
        cout << "Monday" << endl;
        break;

    case FRIDAY:
        cout << "Friday" << endl;
        break;

    default:
        cout << "Another day" << endl;
}

Write decimal 10 as a binary integer literal in C++:

int number = 0b1010;

The variable number contains the decimal value 10.

Write decimal 10 as an octal integer literal in C++:

int number = 012;

The leading 0 tells C++ that 12 should be interpreted as an octal value, so number contains the decimal value 10.

Write decimal 255 as a hexadecimal integer literal in C++:

int number = 0xFF;

The variable number contains the decimal value 255.
